Abstract

Street lighting accounts for a significant share of a municipality's electricity consumption, and lights that stay at full brightness all night waste energy when roads are empty. Manual switching and fixed-timer schedules also make maintenance and fault management slow and inefficient. This project presents an IoT-based automatic street light system built on the ESP8266 microcontroller that combines motion-based dimming with remote control from both a utility office and a mobile application.

The system uses a motion detection sensor to sense vehicles or pedestrians on the road. When movement is detected, the light operates at full brightness, and when no movement is detected for a set period, the ESP8266 automatically reduces the light intensity using PWM-based dimming. This maintains basic visibility while cutting energy use and extending the lifespan of the lamps. The ESP8266's built-in Wi-Fi connects each street light node to the Blynk IoT platform, allowing authorized personnel at the powerline office to switch lights on or off, adjust brightness, and monitor their status from a central location. The same control is available to authorized users through the Blynk mobile app, which also allows manual override when needed.

By combining automatic dimming with centralized remote monitoring, the proposed system reduces power consumption, lowers maintenance costs, and speeds up fault detection and response. The design is low-cost, easy to install, and scalable across streets and colonies, making it a practical step toward smart city and energy-efficient public lighting.
